<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Slow-start vehicle-type override for startup-lost-time experiments.
  Loaded via additional-files when slow_start is true.

  Design choice: tau = 1.8s
  ========================
  In SUMO's Krauss car-following model, tau is the driver's desired minimum
  time headway (seconds). The default is tau=1.0s, which yields an unrealistically
  high saturation flow:

    SUMO default:  headway = tau + minGap/v = 1.0 + 2.5/11.1 ~ 1.23s -> ~2930 veh/h
    With tau=1.8:  headway = 1.8 + 2.5/11.1 ~ 2.03s                -> ~1776 veh/h

  The HCM (Highway Capacity Manual) default saturation flow rate is
  1900 veh/h/ln (headway ~ 1.89s). tau=1.8 brings SUMO within the realistic
  HCM range (1700-2200 veh/h depending on conditions).

  Additional justification:
    - HCM startup lost time default: 2.0s per phase (the total extra time
      for the first ~4 queue vehicles to react and accelerate).
    - Field calibration (Chowdhury & Chakraborty 2024, Khulna): tau=1.4s
      under normal conditions; higher under distraction.
    - ElDessouki & AlManeea (2023): smartphone usage at signals increases
      startup lost time beyond HCM's 2.0s default.
    - Auerbach et al. (arXiv 2307.11357): identifies tau as a key sim-to-real
      transfer gap for TSC policies; default tau=1.0 is idealized.
    - UNECE AEBS report: driver reaction time 0.86-1.39s (normal), higher
      under surprise conditions; 1.8s accounts for perception + decision +
      motor response at queue discharge.

  Effect on traffic:
    - Startup lost time: each vehicle in a queue waits ~1.8s before following
      the one ahead when the light turns green (vs 1.0s default).
    - Reduced saturation flow: effective green time wasted on reaction gaps.
    - Queue discharge takes longer, compounding across intersections.

  Topology agnostic: overrides the "pkw" vType used by all three networks
  (grid4x4, manhattan_28x7, cologne1) without changing route files.
  All other vType params (length, maxSpeed, accel, decel, minGap) stay
  identical to baseline so the only variable is reaction time.

  Agents stay unchanged: they see lane counts/pressure, never tau.
-->
<additional xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
            xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://sumo.dlr.de/xsd/additional_file.xsd">

    <vType id="pkw" length="5.0" width="2.0" minGap="2.5"
           maxSpeed="11.111" accel="2.0" decel="4.5"
           tau="1.8" color="1.0,0.5,0.0"/>
</additional>
